<title>The idea of the chain rule</title>

<p><ref>Video</ref> | <ref>Summary</ref> | <ref>Transcript</ref>  </p>

<description>
An illustration of the basic concept of the chain rule using interactive graphics to diagram the relevant points on the graphs and the corresponding slopes.
</description>

<section><title>Transcript: The idea of the chain rule</title>

<p>Let <m>f</m> be the function defined by <m>f(x)</m> equals <m>e^x</m>, and <m>g</m> be the function defined by <m>g(x)</m> equals <m>-x^2</m>. We’ll define a new function, we’ll call it <m>h(x)</m> to be the composition of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>.
<me>h(x) = f(g(x)).</me>
So, to calculate <m>h(x)</m>, what we need to do is take the input <m>x</m>, first apply it to <m>g</m>, and then take the result and apply it to <m>f</m>. We could also write this composition as <m>f</m> composed with <m>g</m>, and then to specify that the input is <m>x</m> we usually put parentheses around it and write <m>f</m> composed with <m>g(x)</m> like this
<me>h(x) = (f \circ g ) (x).</me></p>
<p>For this definition of <m>f</m> and this definition of <m>g</m>, what is the composition?<m>f</m> composed with <m>g</m>, which we call <m>h(x)</m>. <m>h(x)</m>, which is <m>f(g(x))</m>, equals <m>f</m>, where <m>g(x)</m> is negative <m>x^2</m>. So it's the function <m>f</m>, but rather than using <m>x</m> for the input, we need to use negative <m>x^2</m>. It might be a little confusing because <m>x</m> is used differently in these two parts, so we could write <m>f</m> as <m>f(y) = e^y</m>, or use any other symbol for the input. <m>f</m> is the function that takes its input, and then returns <m>e</m> to the power of that input; it's the exponential function. So <m>f(potato)</m> is <m>e</m> to the <m>potato</m>, and <m>f(-x^2)</m> is just <m>e^(-x^2)</m>. So the composition of <m>e^x</m> with <m>-x^2</m> is <m>e^{-x^2}</m>. So how do you calculate the derivative of this combination? To make it easier, let’s denote <m>f</m> as <m>f(y) = e^y</m>, and then to emphasize that the output of <m>g</m> is the input of <m>f</m>, let’s write <m>y=g^x</m>. Then the input to <m>f</m> is <m>y</m>, and we’ll call its output <m>z</m>, so let's write it as <m>z = f(y)</m>. Now the input to <m>h</m> is the same thing as the input to <m>g</m>, and its output is the same as the output of <m>f</m>, so we’ll call the output of <m>h</m>, <m>z</m>.
<me>y = g(x)\;\;\;\;  z=f(y)\;\;\;\;  z = h(x)</me></p>
<p>Function machines illustrate this pretty well. Here we have the input <m>x</m>, coming into the function <m>g</m>. The output of <m>g</m> is <m>y</m>, which is in the input to <m>f</m> – the second function machine , whose output is then <m>z</m>. The composition <m>h</m> is like the big function machine - each is <m>f</m> composed with <m>g</m>, and it takes the input <m>x</m>, and out comes <m>z</m>. But really the input first goes to <m>g</m>, and then into <m>f</m>.</p> 
<p>The chain rule is a way to determine the derivative of <m>h</m>. The derivative of a composition of two functions. The derivative is the slope of a tangent line, so it's just the ratio of the change in the output over the change in the input, of course in the limit of small changes. So the derivative of <m>h</m> is the change in <m>z</m>, over the change in <m>x</m>. So, it tells you that if you change the input <m>x</m> a little bit, how much does its output, <m>z</m>, change? We often write this derivative as <m>\frac{dz}{dx}</m>. Ok, thats the derivative of <m>h</m>; for the chain rule we want to write this derivative in terms of the derivatives of the component functions, <m>g</m> and <m>f</m>. The derivatives of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are also ratios between the change in output and change in input, The derivative of <m>g</m> is change in its output, which is <m>y</m>, divided by the change in its input, <m>x</m>, which we might write as <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m>, the derivative <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m> is the ratio between the change in <m>y</m> and change in <m>x</m>, in the limit that the change in <m>x</m> is very small. Lastly, the derivative of <m>f</m>, is the change in its output (its output is <m>z</m>), over the change in its input, <m>y</m>, which we can write as <m>\frac{dz}{dy}</m>.</p>
<p>Now here’s the trick of the chain rule. If we multiply the derivatives of <m>g</m> and <m>f</m> together, the change in <m>y</m> from the numerator of <m>g</m>, cancels with the change in <m>y</m> from the denominator of <m>f</m>, so when we multiply we end up with just the change in <m>z</m> over the change in <m>x</m>. But what’s this? That’s just the derivative of <m>h</m>. So the chain rule says that the derivative of <m>h</m> is the product of the derivatives of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>. We could write this as <m>\frac{dz}{dx} = \frac{dz}{dy}</m> times <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m>. In this formula, we denoted the derivative of <m>h</m> by <m>\frac{dz}{dx}</m>. But a more standard way to write the derivative would be to write it as <m>\frac{dh}{dx}</m>. Similarly, we could write the derivative of <m>g</m> as <m>\frac{dg}{dx}</m> rather than <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m>, and the derivative of <m>f</m> as <m>\frac{df}{dy}</m> rather than <m>\frac{dz}{dy}</m>. Using this notation for the derivatives, the chain rule becomes <m>\frac{dh}{dx}</m> equals <m>\frac{df}{dy}</m> times <m>\frac{dg}{dx}</m>. 
<me>\frac{dh}{dx} = \frac{df}{dy} \cdot \frac{dg}{dx}</me>
The derivative of the composition <m>h</m> is the derivative of <m>f</m> times the derivative of <m>g</m>.</p>
<p>We can also write the chain rule using prime notation as <m>h’ = f’</m> times <m>g’</m>
(<m>h’ = f’\cdot g’.</m>)</p> 
<p>There’s one important subtlety that we have ignored so far, and that is: where did we evaluate the derivatives?The derivatives <m>h’</m>, <m>f’</m>, and <m>g’</m> are all functions and in order for the chain rule to be correct, we need to make sure we get the right inputs into each of these functions. We can determine the correct inputs to these functions by looking at the function machine diagrams. The input to <m>h</m> is the variable <m>x</m>, represented by the sphere. So therefore we should evaluate <m>h’</m> at <m>x</m>. The function <m>g</m> is also evaluated at <m>x</m>. So, we should evaluate the derivative <m>g’</m> at <m>x</m>. The function <m>g</m> is also evaluated at <m>x</m>. The function <m>f</m> is different. The sphere represented by <m>x</m> does not go into <m>f</m>. Instead the cube, which represents <m>y</m>, is what goes into <m>f</m>. But what is <m>y</m>? <m>y</m> is the output of <m>g</m> when <m>x</m> goes into <m>g</m>. So, <m>y</m> is <m>g(x)</m>. What goes into the <m>f</m> function machine is <m>g(x)</m> so therefore in the chain rule, we need to evaluate the derivative <m>f’</m> at <m>g</m> of <m>x</m>. Therefore the chain rule for <m>h</m>, the composition of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>, is that <m>h’(x) = f’(g(x))</m> times <m>g’(x)</m>
<me>h'(x) = f'(g(x)) \cdot g'(x)</me> </p>
<p>Let’s go back to the example we had at the beginning. There we had <m>f(x) = e^x</m>, which we’ll think of as <m>f(y) = e^y</m>, and <m>g(x) = - x^2</m>. Then <m>h(x)</m>, or <m>f(g(x))</m> is <m>e^{-x^2}</m>. Now we’ll use the chain rule <m>h’(x) = f’(g(x))</m> times <m>g’(x)</m> to calculate the derivative <m>h’(x)</m>. Let’s calculate the pieces. Since <m>g(x)</m> is negative <m>x^2</m>, <m>g’(x)</m> is <m>-2x</m>. Since <m>f(y)</m> is <m>e^y</m>, the derivative <m>f’(y)</m> is also <m>e^y</m>. Let’s put these pieces into the chain rule. We need to evaluate the derivative <m>f’</m> at <m>g(x)</m> or negative <m>x^2</m>, and then multiply by <m>g’(x)</m>, which is <m>-2x</m>. Next, what is <m>f’(-x^2)</m>? Well, <m>f’(y)</m> is <m>e^y</m>, or <m>f’(potato)</m> is <m>e^{potato}</m>, therefore <m>f’(-x^2)</m> must be <m>e^{-x^2}</m>. When we multiply by <m>-2x</m>, we get the formula for the derivative <m>h’(x)</m>: <m>h’(x)</m> is negative <m>2x</m> times <m>e^{-x^2}</m>. We can also write this as the derivative <m>\frac{d}{dx}</m> of <m>e^{-x^2}</m> is <m>-2x</m> times <m>e^{-x^2}</m>
  <me>\frac{d}{dx} \bigg(e^{-x^2}\bigg) = -2x e^{-x^2}.</me></p>
</section>
